\title{A Finite Infinitesimal Probability Model:\\
Non-Archimedean Measures with Positive Infinitesimal Atoms}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-19}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Classical real-valued probability forbids a fair lottery over infinitely many
equally likely, individually possible outcomes: the Archimedean property of
$\R$ makes any uniform positive probability sum past $1$. We dissolve this
obstruction by relocating probability values into a small, explicit
non-Archimedean ordered ring of infinitesimals, and construct a family of
finitely additive probability measures in which every ``visible'' atom carries
a positive infinitesimal probability $\eps$ while the total mass is exactly
$1$. The value ring is $\LexRat = \Q \times \Q$, with componentwise addition and
a lexicographic order under which $\eps = (0,1)$ is positive yet strictly below
every positive rational. For each parameter $n$, the sample space is
$\mathrm{Option}(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)$: $n$ visible atoms each of weight $\eps$, and
one reservoir atom of weight $1 - n\eps$ absorbing the infinitesimal deficit
while keeping a unit standard component. We establish that $\eps$ is a genuine
infinitesimal, a closed form for the probability of an arbitrary event,
nonnegativity, finite additivity, normalization to $1$, and that each visible
atom has positive infinitesimal probability strictly below $1$. The model is a
minimal, fully verified witness that the ``impossible fair lottery'' is
impossible only over $\R$, and a finite prototype for surreal-valued
probability. We close with conjectures on inclusion--exclusion, non-Archimedean
conditioning, and a standard-part retraction recovering Lebesgue measure.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The impossible lottery}
A recurring foundational embarrassment is the \emph{fair infinite lottery}. We
wish to assign, on an infinite outcome set, a probability in which every outcome
is equally likely and none impossible. Over $\R$ this is unattainable: if each of
countably many outcomes had a common probability $p > 0$, the Archimedean
property yields $m \in \N$ with $mp > 1$, contradicting normalization and
monotonicity. The only uniform assignment compatible with the axioms gives every
singleton probability $0$, severing ``probability $0$'' from ``impossible'':
under the uniform measure on $[0,1]$, every point is null yet the realized
outcome is always some point.

\subsection{The diagnosis: Archimedeanness, not probability}
The obstruction is a property of the value field. $\R$ is \emph{Archimedean}: for
every $\eps > 0$ there is $m \in \N$ with $m\eps > 1$. Hence no positive real can
be the common atomic weight of an infinite uniform lottery. The remedy is to take
values in a non-Archimedean ordered ring with positive \emph{infinitesimals}:
elements $\eps > 0$ with $n\eps < 1$ for all $n \in \N$. Such structures include
the hyperreals, fields of formal Hahn/Laurent series, and Conway's
\emph{surreal numbers}, the universal ordered field in which reals, ordinals, and
infinitesimals coexist.

\subsection{Contribution}
We exhibit the smallest laboratory in which the phenomenon is rigorous and the
arithmetic elementary. We work in $\LexRat = \Q \times \Q$ with lexicographic
order, one infinitesimal $\eps = (0,1)$, and a finite family of measures indexed
by $n$, carried by $\mathrm{Option}(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)$. The central device is the
\emph{reservoir atom}: a single outcome of weight $1 - n\eps$ whose negative
infinitesimal coordinate balances the books, while its unit standard coordinate
keeps it lexicographically positive. We prove every axiom of a finitely additive
probability measure, plus a master closed form from which they all follow.

\section{The value ring of infinitesimals}

\begin{definition}[$\LexRat$]
Let $\LexRat := \Q \times \Q$. Read $x = (x_1, x_2)$ as $x_1 + x_2\eps$ with
$\eps$ a positive infinitesimal symbol. Addition, subtraction, and negation are
componentwise. Distinguished constants: $\mathbf{1} := (1,0)$, $\eps := (0,1)$,
and $\mathrm{ofRat}(q) := (q,0)$ for $q \in \Q$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Lexicographic order]
For $x, y \in \LexRat$:
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{lexLe}(x,y) &\iff x_1 < y_1 \ \lor\ (x_1 = y_1 \land x_2 \le y_2),\\
\mathrm{lexLt}(x,y) &\iff x_1 < y_1 \ \lor\ (x_1 = y_1 \land x_2 < y_2),\\
\Nonneg(x) &\iff \mathrm{lexLe}((0,0), x).
\end{align*}
The standard part $x_1$ dominates; the infinitesimal part $x_2$ breaks ties.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{eps\_pos}]
$\mathrm{lexLt}((0,0),\eps)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Standard parts are equal ($0=0$) and $0 < 1$; take the right disjunct.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[\texttt{eps\_nonneg}]
$\Nonneg(\eps)$. Immediate from \texttt{eps\_pos}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[\texttt{eps\_infinitesimal}]
For every $q \in \Q$ with $0 < q$, $\mathrm{lexLt}(\eps, \mathrm{ofRat}(q))$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Compare $\eps = (0,1)$ and $\mathrm{ofRat}(q) = (q,0)$. Standard parts are $0$
and $q$; since $0 < q$, the left disjunct of $\mathrm{lexLt}$ holds.
\end{proof}

Thus $\eps$ is positive yet strictly below every positive rational. Equivalently
$n\eps = (0,n)$ has standard part $0$, so $n\eps < (1,0) = \mathbf{1}$ for every
$n \in \N$: the Archimedean property fails by design.

\section{The finite infinitesimal probability model}

\subsection{Sample space and atom weights}
Fix $n \in \N$. The sample space is $\Omega_n := \mathrm{Option}(\mathrm{Fin}\,n)$,
with $n$ visible atoms $\mathrm{some}\,i$ ($i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n$) and one
reservoir atom $\mathrm{none}$.

\begin{definition}[\texttt{atomWeight}]
$\mathrm{atomWeight}(n, \mathrm{none}) := (1, -n)$ and
$\mathrm{atomWeight}(n, \mathrm{some}\,i) := (0,1) = \eps$.
\end{definition}

Read as $a + b\eps$: each visible atom has weight $\eps$, the reservoir weight
$1 - n\eps$, parking the deficit $-n\eps$ in its infinitesimal coordinate.

\subsection{The measure}
\begin{definition}[\texttt{prob}]
For an event $A \subseteq \Omega_n$ (a finite set),
$\prob(n, A) := \sum_{x \in A} \mathrm{atomWeight}(n, x)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[\texttt{visiblePart}]
$\visible(n, A) := \{\, i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n : \mathrm{some}\,i \in A \,\}$.
\end{definition}
We have $i \in \visible(n,A) \iff \mathrm{some}\,i \in A$,
$|\visible(n,A)| \le n$, $\visible(n,\mathrm{univ}) = \mathrm{univ}$, and
$|\visible(n,\mathrm{univ})| = n$.

\subsection{The master formula}
\begin{theorem}[\texttt{prob\_eq\_closed\_form}]
For every $A \subseteq \Omega_n$,
\[
\prob(n, A) = \big(\, [\mathrm{none} \in A],\ \ |\visible(n,A)| - [\mathrm{none} \in A]\cdot n \,\big),
\]
where $[P] = 1$ if $P$ holds and $0$ otherwise.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $A$. The empty event gives $(0,0)$, matching the formula. Inserting
$a \notin s$: if $a = \mathrm{none}$, the sum gains $(1,-n)$, the reservoir
indicator flips $0 \to 1$ (raising the standard coordinate to $1$ and subtracting
$n$), and the visible cardinality is unchanged. If $a = \mathrm{some}\,i$ with
$i \notin \visible(n,s)$, the sum gains $\eps$, and
$\visible(n, \mathrm{insert}(\mathrm{some}\,i, s)) = \visible(n,s) \cup \{i\}$
with disjoint union raises the cardinality by $1$. Both cases close by ring
arithmetic.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$\prob(n,\emptyset) = (0,0)$ (\texttt{prob\_empty});
$\prob(n,\{\mathrm{none}\}) = (1,-n)$ (\texttt{prob\_singleton\_none});
$\prob(n,\{\mathrm{some}\,i\}) = \eps$ (\texttt{prob\_singleton\_visible}).
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The probability axioms}
\begin{lemma}[\texttt{atomWeight\_nonneg}]
$\Nonneg(\mathrm{atomWeight}(n,x))$ for all $x \in \Omega_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $\mathrm{none}$, the standard part is $1 > 0$ (left disjunct), despite the
negative infinitesimal coordinate. For $\mathrm{some}\,i$, standard parts are
equal and $0 \le 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonnegativity, \texttt{prob\_nonneg}]
$\Nonneg(\prob(n,A))$ for every event $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the closed form. If $\mathrm{none} \in A$, the standard coordinate is $1 > 0$.
Otherwise it is $0$ and the infinitesimal coordinate is $|\visible(n,A)| \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite additivity, \texttt{prob\_union\_disjoint}]
If $A, B$ are disjoint, $\prob(n, A \cup B) = \prob(n,A) + \prob(n,B)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\prob$ is a finite sum of $\mathrm{atomWeight}$; summation over a disjoint union
splits as $\sum_{A \cup B} = \sum_A + \sum_B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normalization, \texttt{prob\_univ}]
$\prob(n, \mathrm{univ}) = \mathbf{1} = (1,0)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the closed form with $A = \mathrm{univ}$: $\mathrm{none} \in \mathrm{univ}$
gives standard coordinate $1$, and $|\visible(n,\mathrm{univ})| = n$ gives
infinitesimal coordinate $n - n = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Infinitesimal atoms, \texttt{visible\_singleton\_infinitesimal}]
For each $i$, $\prob(n,\{\mathrm{some}\,i\}) = \eps$ and
$\mathrm{lexLt}(\prob(n,\{\mathrm{some}\,i\}), \mathbf{1})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equality is \texttt{prob\_singleton\_visible}; the strict inequality is
\texttt{eps\_infinitesimal} at $q = 1$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reading the model}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Object & Weight $a + b\eps$ & Std.\ part & Inf.\ part \\
\midrule
visible atom $\mathrm{some}\,i$ & $\eps$ & $0$ & $1$ \\
all $n$ visible atoms & $n\eps$ & $0$ & $n$ \\
reservoir $\mathrm{none}$ & $1 - n\eps$ & $1$ & $-n$ \\
whole space $\mathrm{univ}$ & $1$ & $1$ & $0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Every visible atom is possible ($\eps > 0$) and infinitely unlikely
($\eps < q$ for all positive rationals $q$). Finitely many atoms sum to
$n\eps < 1$. Normalization is exact.

\section{Why the classical no-go theorem is not contradicted}
The classical impossibility argument sums infinitely many atomic probabilities
and observes divergence. Two features block it without weakening probability:
(i) additivity is stated for finite disjoint unions, and the natural domain is
the Boolean algebra of finite subsets; (ii) genuine infinitesimals give
$n\eps < 1$ for every $n$. In the broader program the sample space is $[0,1]$,
the value field a non-Archimedean field $K = \mathrm{Lex}(\R\llbracket \Q
\rrbracket)$ (a surrogate for the surreals), and every point gets positive
infinitesimal mass while the whole interval has mass exactly $1$; the paradox
dissolves because the honest domain is the finite-union Boolean algebra, on which
$[0,1]$ is not a disjoint union of its points.

\section{Algorithms}
\textbf{Lexicographic comparison} ($O(1)$): compare standard parts, then
infinitesimal parts. \textbf{Direct probability} ($O(|A|)$): sum
$\mathrm{atomWeight}$ over $A$ componentwise. \textbf{Closed-form probability}
($O(|A|)$): standard coordinate $= [\mathrm{none} \in A]$; infinitesimal
coordinate $= |\{i : \mathrm{some}\,i \in A\}| - [\mathrm{none} \in A]\cdot n$.
Equivalence of the latter two is \texttt{prob\_eq\_closed\_form}.

\section{Applications}
The model is an axiom-checked witness that uniform every-outcome-possible
assignments exist over non-Archimedean values; it restores the distinction
between ``probability $0$'' and ``impossible''; it is a finite, fully verified
prototype for surreal-valued probability; and its standard-part coordinate is the
shadow map of nonstandard analysis, suggesting a clean reduction to classical
measure theory.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Inclusion--exclusion / extension]
The measure extends from disjoint joins to a finitely additive measure on the
Boolean algebra of elementary sets, satisfying
$\mu(E_1 \cup E_2) + \mu(E_1 \cap E_2) = \mu E_1 + \mu E_2$ and monotonicity. The
upgrade works because the standard part (continuous content) and the
infinitesimal part (atomic count) obey inclusion--exclusion in independent graded
pieces of the value ring.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Non-Archimedean conditioning]
For $\mu B \ne 0$, $\mu(A \mid B) := \mu(A \cap B)/\mu B$ is well-defined in the
value field, lies in $[0,1]$, and conditioning on a single point of mass $\eps$
yields a genuine probability, e.g.\ $\mu(\{x\} \mid \{x,y\}) = 1/2$, where
real-valued measures produce $0/0$. Division by $\eps$ is legal in a field, so
ratios of order-$1$ quantities collapse to order-$0$ reals.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Standard-part retraction]
The order-$0$ coefficient map $\mathrm{st} : K \to \R$ is an ordered-ring
retraction sending the infinitesimal measure of every elementary set to its
Lebesgue measure. In the finite model, $\mathrm{st}(\prob(n,A))$ is the first
coordinate: $1$ for the whole space, $0$ for any finite set of points.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Conclusion}
We constructed and verified a finite family of finitely additive probability
measures valued in a non-Archimedean ring of infinitesimals. Every visible atom
carries a positive infinitesimal probability strictly below $1$; the measure is
nonnegative, finitely additive, and exactly normalized; and a single closed form
computes every event. The impossible fair lottery is impossible only over the
Archimedean reals.

